{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וייצר", :translit "va-yitzer", :gloss "and He formed"} {:he "וייצר ה' אלהים את האדם", :translit "va-yitzer HaShem Elokim et ha-adam", :gloss "and the LORD God formed the man"} {:he "וייצר ה' אלהים מן האדמה כל חית השדה", :translit "va-yitzer HaShem Elokim min ha-adamah kol chayat ha-sadeh", :gloss "and the LORD God formed from the ground every beast of the field"} {:he "יצר טוב", :translit "yetzer tov", :gloss "good inclination"} {:he "יצר הרע", :translit "yetzer ha-ra", :gloss "the evil inclination"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Plene spelling וייצר (two yods) for man vs. defective וייצר (one yod) for animals signals two inclinations vs. one"}], :frame {:text "Genesis 2:7 spells וייצר with two yods when God forms man, while Genesis 2:19 uses a single yod for the animals. The doubled letter foreshadows that man alone receives both a good inclination and an evil inclination, while animals possess only one nature. The Talmud identifies the dual spelling as a sign of the two inclinations placed within man (Berakhot 61a).", :sources ["Berakhot 61a" "Genesis 2:7" "Genesis 2:19"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים", :translit "va-yipach be-apav nishmat chayim", :gloss "and He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים", :spells "חותם"}], :frame {:text "The final letters of the phrase ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים spell חותם ('seal'), evoking the sealing or stamping of man with the divine image at creation (Genesis 1:27, Genesis 2:7).", :sources ["Genesis 1:27" "Genesis 2:7"]}} {:quotes [{:he "נשמת", :translit "nishmat", :gloss "breath of"} {:he "ויפח באפיו נשמת", :translit "va-yipach be-apav nishmat", :gloss "and He breathed into his nostrils the breath of"} {:he "כל אשר נשמת רוח חיים", :translit "kol asher nishmat ruach chayim", :gloss "all in whose nostrils is the breath of the spirit of life"} {:he "נר ה' נשמת אדם", :translit "ner HaShem nishma